---
import GuideLayout from "@layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const title = "Building Diminished-Value Market Evidence | FairClaimCalculator";
const description =
  "How to build real market evidence for a diminished-value claim by comparing clean-history and accident-history vehicle listings — a stronger, more specific alternative to a formula alone.";
const path = "/guides/diminished-value/building-diminished-value-market-evidence/";
---
<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  h1="Building Diminished-Value Market Evidence"
  cluster={{ name: "Diminished Value", href: "/guides/diminished-value/" }}
  publishedDate="2026-07-29"
  reviewedDate="2026-07-29"
  summary="Real market evidence compares what buyers actually pay for clean-history vehicles versus otherwise-identical accident-history vehicles in your specific market — a stronger complement to a disclosed formula baseline."
  sources={[
    "Methodology note describing a comparison technique (clean-history vs. accident-history comparable groups), not a citation to an external legal source.",
  ]}
  relatedLinks={[
    { label: "Diminished Value Baseline calculator", href: "/calculators/diminished-value-baseline/" },
    { label: "The 17c Formula's Real History, Calculation, and Limits", href: "/guides/diminished-value/the-17c-formula-history-calculation-and-limits/" },
    { label: "Finding Your Own Comparable Vehicles", href: "/guides/total-loss/finding-your-own-comparable-vehicles/" },
  ]}
>
  <h2>The core idea</h2>
  <p>
    Instead of relying only on a formula, gather two groups of real listings for your same
    year/make/model/trim: vehicles with a <strong>clean title history</strong>, and vehicles with a
    <strong>disclosed accident/repair history</strong> (visible on a Carfax/AutoCheck-style report).
    Compare the two groups' prices directly — the gap between them is real market evidence of
    diminished value for your specific vehicle, in your specific market.
  </p>

  <h2>How to build a usable comparison</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Search for 4 or more listings in each group if possible — clean-history and accident-history.</li>
    <li>Match year, mileage range, and trim as closely as you can between the two groups.</li>
    <li>Record price, mileage, and a link/date-checked for every listing.</li>
    <li>Enter both groups into the <a href="/calculators/diminished-value-baseline/">Diminished Value Baseline calculator</a>'s market-evidence worksheet.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="example-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Worked example.</strong> Clean-history group: 5 listings, median $21,400. Accident-history
      group: 4 listings, median $19,600. The median gap of $1,800 is real evidence for your specific
      market — distinct from, and potentially different than, a 17c-style formula result for the
      same car. The calculator only reports a median gap when both groups have a sufficient sample
      size; with fewer listings, it labels the comparison as limited rather than presenting a false
      sense of precision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why sample size matters</h2>
  <p>
    A comparison built from one listing in each group could easily reflect those two sellers'
    pricing quirks rather than a real market pattern. More listings per group make the median and
    range more representative — which is why the calculator flags small samples as limited evidence
    rather than a confident conclusion.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this is never averaged with the 17c-style baseline</h2>
  <p>
    The formula baseline and the market-evidence comparison answer different questions — one is a
    disclosed, standardized calculation; the other is what your specific local market actually
    shows. Averaging them would produce a number that misrepresents both. The calculator always
    displays them as two separate, clearly labeled outputs.
  </p>

  <h2>What this guide cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    It cannot tell you how many comparable listings are "enough" for your specific insurer's
    process, or guarantee that a market gap you find will be accepted as your claim's value — only
    that it's stronger evidence than an unsupported assertion.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
